@startuml requirement-issue-states
' cumin works - states of a requirement issue.
' A state is a label on GitHub. Each arrow names one row of issue-states.md (R1 to R7).
' Render: run scripts/render-diagrams.sh at the root of the repository.

hide empty description

state "Draft\n(no status label)" as Draft
state "cumin/status/ready" as Ready
state "cumin/status/planning" as Planning
state "cumin/status/awaiting-owner-review\n(check the sub-issues)" as PlanReview
state "cumin/status/implementing" as Implementing
state "cumin/status/awaiting-owner-review\n(acceptance)" as Acceptance
state "cumin/status/awaiting-owner-decision" as Decision

[*] --> Draft : Owner writes the requirement,\nadds cumin/type/requirement
Draft --> Ready : Owner finishes,\nadds cumin/status/ready
Draft --> Implementing : R3 Owner wrote the sub-issues,\nadded cumin/status/ready to one
Ready --> Planning : R1 cumin asks the Planner to split
Planning --> PlanReview : R2 split done
Planning --> Implementing : R2 every sub-issue closed\n(resume after a blocked acceptance check)
Planning --> Decision : R2 split failed
Decision --> Ready : Owner fixes the requirement,\nadds cumin/status/ready
PlanReview --> Implementing : R3 Owner checked, added\ncumin/status/ready to a sub-issue
Implementing --> Implementing : R4 all sub-issues closed,\nPlanner checks acceptance
Implementing --> Decision : R4 the check returned blocked
Implementing --> Acceptance : R7 acceptance comment posted
Implementing --> PlanReview : R6 only sub-issues\nwithout a status label remain
Acceptance --> Implementing : R3 Owner sends it back\n(adds a sub-issue with cumin/status/ready)
Acceptance --> [*] : R5 Owner closes the requirement issue\n(= accepted)

@enduml
